{
    "title": "Transfer of the Functions of the Tax Division to the Civil Division and the Criminal Division",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 10, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-10/pdf/2025-22449.pdf",
    "summary": "Big changes from Washington this week touch air travel, tax enforcement, and civil rights. In short: transportation officials won\u2019t enforce passenger-service promises when flights are disrupted by urgent safety fixes; the Justice Department is folding its tax work into other divisions; and it\u2019s narrowing its civil rights rules to cover only intentional discrimination under Title VI.\n\nFirst, the Department of Transportation said it will not take action against airlines that don\u2019t provide meals, hotels, or other perks promised in their customer service plans when delays or cancellations happen because of emergency safety orders that require immediate, unscheduled repairs before a flight. Airlines still have to report these disruptions under existing rules, and many may choose to help customers anyway. But this guidance means fewer penalties tied to events that airlines argue are safety-driven and outside their control.\n\nWho benefits and who doesn\u2019t here? Airlines gain clarity and relief from some customer-service obligations during urgent safety events, which could help operations recover faster. Passengers, however, may see fewer guaranteed amenities when a flight is scrubbed for last-minute safety work, even if the airline could have covered meals or hotels in the past.\n\nSecond, the Department of Justice is dissolving its standalone Tax Division and moving its work elsewhere. Civil tax cases will be handled by the Civil Division, and criminal tax cases will go to the Criminal Division. The Department says this internal reshuffle is about streamlining and efficiency. If it works as intended, taxpayers and the courts could see simpler lines of authority and faster decisions. On the other hand, the change may unsettle staff and could dilute the specialized focus that a dedicated tax unit provided, at least in the short term.\n\nThird, the Department of Justice is revising its Title VI regulations so they prohibit only intentional discrimination by recipients of federal funds, not policies that are neutral on their face but end up hurting certain racial or ethnic groups more than others. Supporters say this better matches the statute and reduces compliance costs. Critics warn it removes a key tool for addressing systemic inequities in schools, transit, health care, and other federally funded programs.\n\nTaken together, these moves reduce some regulatory exposure for airlines and federal funding recipients, and promise internal efficiencies at Justice. The trade-offs include fewer guaranteed protections for travelers and a narrower path for civil rights enforcement, while the tax reorganization tests whether consolidation can deliver the efficiency gains it promises.",
